---
const faqs = [
    {
        question: "What is a capping chuck?",
        answer: "A capping chuck is the part of a capping machine that grabs a bottle cap and screws it on. It sits at the bottom of a spinning shaft called a spindle. It turns the cap onto the bottle threads and lets go when the cap reaches a set torque (twisting force). Chucks are used on both rotary and inline cappers."
    },
    {
        question: "Is there a difference between capping heads and chucks?",
        answer: "Yes. The chuck is only the part that grips the cap. The capping head, also called a headset, is the whole unit. It includes the chuck, the clutch that controls torque, and the part that attaches to the spindle. On ROPP lines for aluminum caps, the head also has thread-forming rollers and a top pressure pad. People often mix up the two words."
    },
    {
        question: "How do you calibrate a capping chuck?",
        answer: "Start with the torque number from the cap maker. On a friction (spring) clutch, turn the threaded collar or change the shims to set spring pressure. On a magnetic clutch, change the DC current to the coil. Then cap 30 or more bottles, test each one with a calibrated torque tester, and check that the spread (CV%) is 5% or less. High-speed rotary lines aim for 3% or less."
    },
    {
        question: "Do you need the cap liner specs to set up a chuck?",
        answer: "Yes. The liner, or the built-in seal on a linerless cap, changes how much torque and top load it takes to seal. A foil induction liner and a plain plastic seal do not need the same settings. Get the torque spec from the cap maker for the exact cap and liner you run."
    },
    {
        question: "What causes uneven torque on a capping line?",
        answer: "The usual causes are worn clutch plates, a dirty magnetic disc, unsteady air pressure, and caps or bottles that are out of spec. Play (backlash) where the chuck connects to the spindle, or a chuck bore that is the wrong size, can also cause it."
    },
    {
        question: "Can aftermarket capping chucks replace OEM parts?",
        answer: "Sometimes. The machine brand alone does not tell you if a part will fit. Check the machine model, the spindle connection, the cap type, and any approved change parts on the line before you order."
    },
    {
        question: "What type of capping chuck is best for high-speed lines?",
        answer: "Magnetic (hysteresis) chucks are best on rotary lines above 800 caps per minute, or anywhere torque spread must stay under 3%. Friction chucks fit inline and slower rotary lines where cost matters more. The right pick depends on the cap, the line speed, and how much torque variation you can accept."
    },
];
---

<section id="faq" class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
        <span class="kicker kicker-blue">FAQ</span>
        <h2>Common questions</h2>

        <div class="cards cards-2">
            {faqs.map((faq) => (
                <div class="card">
                    <h3>{faq.question}</h3>
                    <p>{faq.answer}</p>
                </div>
            ))}
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- FAQPage Schema -->
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": faq.question,
        "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": faq.answer
        }
    }))
})} />
